.TH LIBMORSEDEC 3 "2026-10-16" "libmorsedec 0.2.0" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
libmorsedec \- streaming multichannel Morse code decoding library
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <morsedec.h>

cc program.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs morsedec)
.fi
.PP
For static linking, request the transitive FFTW3 and math dependencies:
.PP
.nf
cc program.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs --static morsedec)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libmorsedec
is a C11 library that discovers and tracks CW carriers and decodes their
Morse elements, characters and word boundaries.
Each decoder has independent stream state and bounded per-channel messages.
The application supplies mono audio, complex I/Q samples, or calibrated
linear-power spectra, then polls channel and message snapshots.
.PP
The library does not open audio or SDR devices, read files, resample input,
start background threads, or deliver callbacks.
Processing is synchronous in the caller's thread.
The FFT frontend uses FFTW3 double precision.
The public header supports C and C++ linkage.
.SH FUNCTION INDEX
Every public function has its own section 3 manual page.
.SS Configuration and lifetime
.TP
.BR md_config_init (3)
Initialize the configuration with audio defaults.
.TP
.BR md_new (3)
Construct a mono audio decoder.
.TP
.BR md_new_iq (3)
Construct an I+jQ decoder with signed IF frequencies.
.TP
.BR md_delete (3)
Release a handle and its FFT plan and buffers.
.TP
.BR md_reset (3)
Discard stream state while preserving configuration and storage.
.SS Streaming input
.TP
.BR md_process (3)
Feed normalized mono float samples.
.TP
.BR md_process_s16 (3)
Feed mono signed 16-bit samples.
.TP
.BR md_process_iq (3)
Feed normalized float I,Q pairs.
.TP
.BR md_process_iq_s16 (3)
Feed signed 16-bit I,Q pairs.
.TP
.BR md_process_spectrum (3)
Feed one calibrated retained-power frame per call.
.TP
.BR md_flush (3)
Drain pending output and seal the stream.
.SS Geometry and channels
.TP
.BR md_get_geometry (3)
Copy the actual FFT grid and frame timing.
.TP
.BR md_get_channel_count (3)
Count tracked channels.
.TP
.BR md_get_channel (3)
Copy a channel summary by enumeration position.
.TP
.BR md_get_state (3)
Query the current channel's decoder state.
.TP
.BR md_get_frequency (3)
Query the current channel's frequency or signed IF offset.
.TP
.BR md_get_snr (3)
Query the current channel's relative SNR estimate.
.TP
.BR md_get_wpm (3)
Query the current channel's Morse speed estimate.
.TP
.BR md_get_frame_count (3)
Count completed spectrum frames, including flush padding.
.SS Frequency selection
.TP
.BR md_pin_frequency (3)
Select and pin a frequency, including 0 Hz IF.
.TP
.BR md_unpin_frequency (3)
Release the current pin.
.TP
.BR md_set_frequency (3)
Pin a nonzero frequency, or unpin when the argument is zero.
.SS Messages
.TP
.BR md_get_characters (3)
Borrow a read-only message snapshot with character metadata.
.TP
.BR md_get_text (3)
Copy a NUL-terminated text snapshot into caller storage.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Initialize a
.B struct morsedec_config
with
.BR md_config_init (3)
before changing selected fields.
A wholly zero-initialized structure is not a valid configuration.
Both constructors copy the structure; the caller retains ownership.
Configuration cannot be changed on an existing handle.
.TP
.B uint32_t sample_rate
Input mono samples per second, or complex pairs per second for I/Q.
Default 48000; must be nonzero.
There is no internal resampling.
.TP
.B size_t fft_size
Even FFT length N, at least 64 and no greater than INT_MAX.
Zero selects 2*floor(sample_rate*0.064+0.5), approximately 128 ms.
Default zero.
.TP
.B size_t hop_size
Input samples or pairs per spectrum frame.
Must be at least one, no greater than N, and represent 5 through 20 ms.
Zero selects floor(sample_rate*0.010625+0.5).
Default zero.
.TP
.B double min_frequency
Retained lower band edge in Hz.
Audio requires a nonnegative edge; I/Q permits signed offsets from 0 Hz IF,
no lower than -sample_rate/2.
Default 200 from md_config_init().
.TP
.B double max_frequency
Exclusive retained upper edge in Hz, greater than min_frequency and no greater
than sample_rate/2.
Both edges must be finite.
Default 1200.
.TP
.B size_t max_channels
Number of preallocated channel slots; must be positive.
Default 32.
No additional slots are allocated during streaming.
.TP
.B double initial_wpm
Zero selects the duration model's default priors, approximately 23 WPM.
A finite value from 5 through 80 shifts those priors toward the requested
speed.
This is not a fixed speed or a guaranteed supported decode range.
Default zero.
.PP
The rounded band must contain at least 32 retained bins.
The decoder needs ten bins of guard space at each edge for tracking and
spectral-shape analysis, so include margins around desired signals.
At the default spacing of 7.8125 Hz, each margin is 78.125 Hz.
.PP
.B md_new(NULL)
uses the audio defaults above.
.B md_new_iq(NULL)
uses the same defaults except for min_frequency=-1200.
For an explicit I/Q configuration, set the desired signed edges yourself;
md_config_init() always initializes audio defaults.
.SH DATA TYPES
.SS Decoder handle
.B morsedec_decoder_t
is an opaque owned handle.
Only md_new() and md_new_iq() create it; only md_delete() releases it.
Internal helpers share the md_ prefix but are not public interfaces.
Use only functions declared in morsedec.h.
.SS Geometry
.B struct morsedec_geometry
is a caller-owned copy returned by md_get_geometry().
Its fields are:
.TP
.B uint32_t sample_rate
Effective samples or complex pairs per second.
.TP
.B size_t fft_size, hop_size, spectrum_size
Actual FFT length, hop length and retained bin count.
.TP
.B double first_frequency
Frequency of retained bin zero in Hz; signed IF offset for I/Q.
.TP
.B double bin_spacing
sample_rate/fft_size in Hz.
Bin j is first_frequency+j*bin_spacing.
.TP
.B double frame_seconds
hop_size/sample_rate in seconds.
.PP
Requested edges map to bin numbers using floor(frequency*N/sample_rate+0.5).
The upper edge is excluded.
The I/Q grid includes negative Nyquist when requested, and excludes positive
Nyquist so the same complex bin is not represented twice.
Query geometry to obtain the actual rounded band.
.SS Channel summary
.B struct morsedec_channel
is a caller-owned copy returned by md_get_channel().
.TP
.B uint64_t id
Stable nonzero identity until the channel retires or the decoder is reset.
IDs are not enumeration indices and are not reused across resets of a handle.
IDs from different handles are independent.
.TP
.B enum morsedec_state state
Decoder state listed below.
.TP
.B double frequency
Tracked FFT-bin center in Hz.
For I/Q, a signed offset from the stream IF center at 0 Hz.
.TP
.B double snr
Internal signal-to-noise estimate in dB, derived from confident delayed marks.
It is not a calibrated RF measurement and may be unrepresentative at startup.
.TP
.B double wpm
Current duration-model speed estimate using the PARIS timing convention.
.TP
.B double key_probability
Current soft keying observation probability, between zero and one.
.TP
.B double strength
Internal dimensionless envelope-strength indicator, not dB or RF power.
.TP
.B size_t character_count
Number of records in the current bounded message snapshot.
.SS Decoder states
.TP
.B MORSEDEC_STATE_NONE
No current channel. Returned by md_get_state(), not a live channel summary.
.TP
.B MORSEDEC_STATE_PAUSE
Automatically selected channel in a pause or acquisition state.
.TP
.B MORSEDEC_STATE_DECODING
Automatically selected channel in a decoding state.
.TP
.B MORSEDEC_STATE_LOCKED_PAUSE
Pinned channel in a pause or acquisition state.
.TP
.B MORSEDEC_STATE_LOCKED_DECODING
Pinned channel in a decoding state.
.PP
State describes the channel decoder, not instantaneous key-up or key-down.
A paused channel can have a nonempty message.
.SS Character record
.B struct morsedec_character
contains the following fields:
.TP
.B unsigned char character
Latin-1 character byte.
Prosigns use lowercase pairs such as ar and sk, with one record per byte.
An undecodable run may produce an asterisk.
.TP
.B enum morsedec_word_type word_type
Word interpretation label: MORSEDEC_WORD_GENERIC, MORSEDEC_WORD_DICTIONARY,
MORSEDEC_WORD_REPORT, MORSEDEC_WORD_PROSIGN, MORSEDEC_WORD_QRL,
or MORSEDEC_WORD_CALLSIGN.
Labels may be revised by later observations.
.TP
.B uint64_t ordinal
Position on the source channel timebase, not a character count or array index.
The implementation reserves 256 positions per source frame for multiple
characters from a prosign.
Use the ordinal together with the handle and channel ID to apply revisions.
.TP
.B int low_snr
Nonzero when the decoded character includes low-SNR evidence.
.TP
.B double word_space_probability
Word-boundary evidence; 2.0 is a forced boundary marker, not a probability.
.TP
.B int word_end
Number of spaces after the character: zero, one, or two.
.SH STREAMING MODEL
Construct a handle, optionally pin a carrier, feed successive input chunks,
and consume channel snapshots after each processing call.
At end of input call md_flush(), consume final snapshots, then delete the
handle
or reset it for a new stream.
.PP
The first successful processing call selects either sample input or external
spectrum input until reset.
Even a zero-count sample call selects sample mode.
An audio handle accepts both mono sample formats; an I/Q handle accepts both
I/Q formats.
Changing between float and signed 16-bit within that handle type needs no
reset.
Changing between sample input and spectrum input requires md_reset().
Changing between audio and I/Q requires a new handle.
.PP
Input arrays are borrowed only during the processing call.
No input pointer is retained.
Arbitrary complete-sample or complete-pair chunks are accepted; incomplete
hops
are retained inside the decoder.
The API accepts C values in native representation, not a byte stream.
Byte framing, byte order and incomplete I/Q pairs are the host's
responsibility.
.PP
Float components are clamped to [-1,1], with non-finite values replaced by
zero.
Signed 16-bit components are divided by 32768.
Processing performs no heap allocation, blocking I/O or callbacks.
This does not imply a hard real-time execution bound: channel creation,
historical replay and model updates make work vary by frame.
.PP
The decoder has no input timestamp or discontinuity API.
After dropped samples, sample-rate changes or receiver retuning, reset or
recreate the appropriate handle rather than silently joining unrelated input.
.SS I/Q frequency convention
Interleaved input is I0,Q0,I1,Q1,... representing I+jQ.
Counts passed to md_process_iq() or md_process_iq_s16() are complex pairs,
not scalar components or bytes.
For I=cos(2*pi*f*t), Q=sin(2*pi*f*t), the reported offset is +f Hz.
Negative-frequency carriers remain distinct from positive-frequency carriers.
Swapping I and Q reverses the orientation.
.PP
No receiver center frequency is stored in the decoder.
To display RF frequency, the host adds its RF center to the signed offset
according to its receiver's frequency convention.
The decoder performs no DC removal or I/Q-imbalance correction.
.SS External spectra
md_process_spectrum() consumes exactly one linear-power frame with
geometry.spectrum_size bins per call.
The caller supplies frames at geometry.frame_seconds intervals on the same
ascending grid, window alignment and power scale as the frontend.
Audio power is 0.001*abs(FFT(window*audio))^2;
I/Q power is 0.00025*abs(FFT(window*(I+jQ)))^2.
See md_process_spectrum(3) for the complete window and calibration formula.
Decibels or arbitrary waterfall pixels are not compatible power frames.
Non-finite and nonpositive power values become zero.
.SS Flush and reset
md_flush() pads a partial hop and drains about one second of silence.
It commits pending output and seals the stream.
Repeated flush is a successful no-op.
Processing and pin/unpin operations fail after flush until reset;
queries remain available.
Frame counts include the synthetic drain frames.
.PP
md_reset() discards output, pins, channel identities and learned stream state,
and clears the sample/spectrum mode selection.
Geometry, handle type and allocated storage remain available for reuse.
md_delete() does not implicitly flush or preserve output.
.SH CHANNELS AND SELECTION
Discovery and tracking process multiple carriers within the retained band,
up to max_channels slots.
Enumerate with md_get_channel_count() and md_get_channel(); retain IDs instead
of enumeration indices because ordering can change during processing.
All live channels can be queried independently of the current selection.
.PP
The automatic current-channel policy waits for duration-model training and
compares channel power with hysteresis.
It does not select every observed peak immediately.
To receive a short known-frequency signal without waiting for automatic
selection, pin a frequency.
.PP
md_pin_frequency() rounds and clamps requests to the retained band's usable
interior, reusing a nearby channel or allocating a preallocated slot.
It selects that channel, prevents automatic retirement and current-channel
switching, and leaves discovery of other channels enabled.
Pitch tracking continues; pinning is not frequency translation or an exact
frequency hold.
.PP
Use md_pin_frequency(decoder, 0) to pin the IF center.
md_set_frequency(decoder, 0) instead releases the pin, as does
md_unpin_frequency().
Releasing a pin initially retains the channel and message, but future
processing can switch or retire it.
.SH MESSAGE SNAPSHOTS
Messages are bounded, revisable snapshots, not queues of new characters.
md_get_text() returns Latin-1 text with word_end spaces;
md_get_characters() exposes the records and their source ordinals.
Reading a message does not consume or clear it.
.PP
Channel ID zero selects the current channel.
If no current channel exists, this yields a successful empty message.
A nonzero unknown or retired channel ID yields ENOENT.
The maximum is MORSEDEC_MESSAGE_CAPACITY, currently 500 records.
When full, old records are discarded in blocks; retired channels no longer
expose messages.
Long-running applications must archive output while processing.
.PP
Copied channel summaries and copied text belong to the caller.
A borrowed character array must not be modified or freed and is valid only
until the next mutating call on its handle.
Copy it before processing, flush, reset, selection changes or deletion.
Keep the handle serialized across size-query/copy or enumeration operations.
.PP
To retain a transcript, key observations by channel ID and ordinal and apply
later record revisions.
Blindly appending every text snapshot duplicates output and loses revisions.
After a reset, start a fresh stream archive; do not compare ordinals from
unrelated channel timebases as if they were one character sequence.
.SH RETURN VALUES
Fallible int functions return MORSEDEC_SUCCESS (1) or MORSEDEC_FAILURE (0).
Constructors return an owned handle or NULL.
Failures set errno; errno is not a success indicator and need not be cleared
on successful calls.
.PP
md_config_init() and md_delete() return void and accept NULL as a no-op.
The scalar queries have absence defaults and no errno-based error return:
md_get_state(NULL) returns MORSEDEC_STATE_NONE;
md_get_channel_count(NULL) and md_get_frame_count(NULL) return zero;
md_get_frequency(NULL), md_get_snr(NULL) and md_get_wpm(NULL) return -1.
The same -1 result applies when there is no current channel.
.PP
Because negative frequencies and negative SNR estimates can be valid,
check md_get_state() or enumerate channels to detect absence.
A failed call's output arguments are unspecified unless its manual page
explicitly states otherwise.
.SH ERRORS
The individual function pages describe which errors apply.
.TP
.B EINVAL
Invalid configuration, argument, input type or mode; an out-of-range
enumeration
index; or a processing/selection operation after flush.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
Decoder storage allocation or FFT planning failed during construction.
.TP
.B ENOENT
A requested nonzero channel ID is no longer tracked.
.TP
.B ENOSPC
No free channel slot for pinning, or insufficient text-buffer capacity.
On text-buffer failure, md_get_text() reports the required byte count,
including NUL, and writes no partial string.
.SH THREAD SAFETY
Serialize all operations on the same handle, including getters and deletion.
Different handles may process concurrently after construction.
The application must also serialize construction and destruction against
all FFTW planner and plan-destruction operations, including those outside
libmorsedec.
.PP
There are no library-owned worker threads, locks for shared handle access,
or callbacks.
Functions are not specified as async-signal-safe.
For signal-driven shutdown, set an application flag and flush/delete from
normal program flow.
.SH EXAMPLE
This complete program reads native-endian float I,Q pairs from stdin and
prints all nonempty channel snapshots with signed IF offsets.
It prints full snapshots, including repeated/revised text; replace the output
helper with an ordinal-aware archive when building a persistent transcript.
Use the streaming APIs directly for device input, or use morsedec-iqlive for
little-endian raw byte streams.
.PP
.nf
#include <morsedec.h>
#include <stdio.h>

static int snapshots(morsedec_decoder_t *d)
{
    char text[3 * MORSEDEC_MESSAGE_CAPACITY + 1];
    size_t required;

    for (size_t i = 0; i < md_get_channel_count(d); i++) {
        struct morsedec_channel c;
        if (!md_get_channel(d, i, &c) ||
            !md_get_text(d, c.id, text, sizeof(text), &required))
            return 0;
        if (text[0])
            printf("%+.3f Hz #%llu: %s\en", c.frequency,
                   (unsigned long long)c.id, text);
    }
    return !ferror(stdout);
}

int main(void)
{
    struct morsedec_config config;
    morsedec_decoder_t *d;
    float iq[2048];
    size_t n;
    int status = 1;

    md_config_init(&config);
    config.sample_rate = 48000; /* complex pairs per second */
    config.min_frequency = -12000;
    config.max_frequency = 12000;
    d = md_new_iq(&config);
    if (!d) {
        perror("md_new_iq");
        return 1;
    }
    while ((n = fread(iq, 1, sizeof(iq), stdin)) != 0) {
        if (n % (2 * sizeof(iq[0]))) {
            fputs("Incomplete I/Q pair\en", stderr);
            goto done;
        }
        if (!md_process_iq(d, iq, n / (2 * sizeof(iq[0])))) {
            perror("md_process_iq");
            goto done;
        }
        if (!snapshots(d))
            goto done;
    }
    if (ferror(stdin)) {
        perror("stdin");
        goto done;
    }
    if (!md_flush(d)) {
        perror("md_flush");
        goto done;
    }
    if (!snapshots(d) || fflush(stdout))
        goto done;
    status = 0;
done:
    md_delete(d);
    return status;
}
.fi
.PP
For mono audio, use md_new() with the nonnegative audio band and replace
the I/Q processing call with md_process(d, samples, sample_count).
For signed 16-bit audio or I/Q, use the corresponding s16 function.
.SH NOTES
Default discovery warms up for about 2.125 seconds and replays bounded
history.
Decoding latency is roughly one second plus acquisition and model learning,
depending on the signal and configured cadence.
Flush adds synthetic time.
.PP
Signals within about three default-grid bins can merge.
Fast 30-40 WPM CW, fading, interference, onset clipping and short handoffs
remain areas of limited validation.
Do not infer a guaranteed range from the accepted initial_wpm parameter.
The existing three-recording corpus was used for tuning and is not an
independent accuracy evaluation.
.SH VERSION
This manual describes libmorsedec 0.2.0, shared-library ABI 1.
All public function names use md_.
Version 0.2.0 replaced the original morsedec_ function symbols without
aliases;
update call sites and dynamic lookups and rebuild consumers.
The header, public data-type names and MORSEDEC_ constants retain their names.
MORSEDEC_VERSION is the compile-time version string, not a runtime query.
